<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="timber-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="timber-title">Timber dealers: tax collected on sale</h2>
      <p>Section 394(1) of the Income-tax Act, 2025 lists sale of timber among the receipts on which a seller collects tax, and the Act as enacted shows 2%. There are conditions and exceptions, including for certain buyers who give a declaration. Rates in this section were revised by the Finance Act, 2026, and we have not confirmed the current timber rate at source.</p>
      <p class="para">So ask two things: does this apply to what you sell, and is it being collected, deposited and reported correctly? Plywood, laminate and veneer are different goods, so do not assume the same answer.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Showroom and yard stock</h2>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Count display pieces as stock, even if they are never moved</li>
        <li>Note timber in different sizes, by cubic measure and value</li>
        <li>Keep offcuts and waste in a simple record, along with any waste sold</li>
        <li>Mark damaged or dated pieces, and value them honestly</li>
        <li>Separate goods held for customers who have already paid</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="carp-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="carp-title">When you send goods out for work</h2>
      <p>If a registered business sends inputs to an outside workshop without paying tax, section 143 of the CGST Act applies. The principal must keep proper accounts, and inputs must be back or supplied from there within one year. Otherwise they are treated as supplied on the day they were sent.</p>
      <p class="para">Ask whether this fits your arrangement, and how to treat payments to carpenters for TDS and labour purposes.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Interior contracts</h2>
      <p>Showrooms sometimes take on a whole interior job, with design, materials and fitting. That can change the GST picture, the point at which income arises, and any tax a business customer deducts from your bill. Raise it before quoting, and put the scope in writing.</p>
      <p class="para">See {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-manufacturers/", "accounting for manufacturers" %} for work in progress, and {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-traders/", "accounting for traders" %} for party credit.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="area-title">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h2 id="area-title" class="first-h2">About the area, and about us</h2>
    <p>Delhi's Department of Industries lists Kirti Nagar as an industrial area in its West zone. The trade press reports a timber market dealers' association with around 500 members, and a city guide describes buyers there having wood or ply made to order. The Delhi government's 2022-23 budget named Kirti Nagar among markets to redevelop, and we have not confirmed the present status of that plan.</p>
    <p>AccountOrbit has no office in Kirti Nagar and does not publish who is available here. Being named on this page does not mean a professional is based nearby.</p>
  </div>
</section>

